<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bemba Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Chitimukulu on the plateau of north-eastern Zambia, which grew powerful through warfare and the 19th-century ivory and slave trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bemba tradition traces the royal Crocodile clan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo basin</a>, from which its founders migrated east and settled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubemba">Lubemba</a> plateau. The paramount title Chitimukulu was established by the early 18th century, with related chiefs governing surrounding territories.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chitimukulu was a sacred ruler, with royal relics and the advice of hereditary priest-councillors (bakabilo), while senior chiefs such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwamba-bemba-title">Mwamba</a> held great power. Farming used the chitemene system of burning cut branches to fertilise poor soils. In the 19th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chileshe-chepela">Chileshe Chepela</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitapankwa">Chitapankwa</a>, the Bemba expanded by raiding and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and captives with Swahili-Arab merchants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Succession disputes after the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitimukulu-sampa">Chitimukulu Sampa</a> in 1896 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwamba-bemba-title">Mwamba</a> in 1898 allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> to take control by 1899. The Chitimukulu remains the Bemba paramount chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1700 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:3.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chileshe Chepela, Chitapankwa, Sampa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Bemba religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bemba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chitimukulu, Bemba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=bemba-kingdom" aria-label="Bemba Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5402 2723 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bemba Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5710 2709h0M5863 2875h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5722 2979h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5722 2979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Zambia. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=bemba-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
